• Revenue: The total amount of money a business brings in from selling its goods or services before any costs or expenses are subtracted. 
It sits at the "top line" of an income statement.

• Income: A broader term that often means net income (the money left over after subtracting all operating expenses, 
taxes, and interest from revenue), or gross income (revenue minus the direct cost of goods sold). 
It can also include non-operating gains like rent or asset sales.

• Profit: The actual financial gain or surplus remaining after all expenses are deducted from total revenue. 
It is the "bottom line" and shows whether a business is truly making

Interest and Growth

• Simple Interest (I): I = P × r × t (where P = principal, r = annual interest rate, t = time in years)
• Simple Total Amount (A): \(A = P(1 + r \times t)\)
• Compound Interest (Future Value, FV): \(FV = PV \times (1 + \frac{r}{n})^{n \times t}\) (where PV = present value, n = compounding periods per year)
• Present Value (PV): \(PV = \frac{FV}{(1 + r)^t}\)
• Effective Annual Rate (EAR): \(EAR = (1 + \frac{r}{n})^n - 1\)

